00:30:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: We’ve actually already “run out” of IPv4 addresses—the last blocks of new, free IPv4 addresses were allocated by regional registries between 2011 and 2019. But the internet didn’t stop working. Here’s what really happens (and has been happening): 1. **Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)** Internet Service Providers (ISPs) put many customers behind a single public IPv4 address. So you share an IP with hundreds or thousands of other users. This works for most web browsing, but can cause issues with online gaming, VoIP, and peer-to-peer connections (like some video calls or torrents). 2. **NAT (Network Address Translation) in homes and businesses** Most home routers already use NAT to allow multiple devices (phones, laptops, smart TVs) to share one public IP. This is normal, but as IPv4 runs out, more layers of NAT are stacked, making direct connections trickier. 3. **IPv4 addresses become a traded commodity** Instead of getting free addresses from a registry, companies buy and sell them on a secondary market. Prices have risen from a few dollars per address to over $40–$60 each. Startups or new ISPs must either buy expensive IPv4 blocks or rely entirely on IPv6 and CGNAT. 4. **IPv6 adoption accelerates** IPv6 has enough addresses for every device on Earth. When IPv4 first ran dry, many networks hesitated. Now, major ISPs (like Comcast, T-Mobile, AT&T in the US, and many in India, Europe) have large IPv6 deployments. Mobile networks especially push IPv6 since every phone needs an address. 5. **Some services become inaccessible or degraded** If you’re behind CGNAT, you can still **access** the internet, but hosting a server (e.g., a home Plex server, a Minecraft server, security cameras) from behind CGNAT is hard unless you use tunneling services (like Cloudflare Tunnel) or buy a static public IP from your ISP. 6. **The internet doesn’t collapse** The core routing (BGP) still works. Public IPv4 addresses are still routed. There is no “the internet stops.” Instead, it becomes more complex: more NAT, more address sharing, more reliance on IPv6. **In short:** We are already living with a “run out” scenario. It means higher costs, more NAT, slower IPv6 uptake for some, and occasional compatibility headaches. The internet continues to function, but the “free public IP per device” model is gone forever.
